      time: '60 s',
      say: 'These curves are measured inside the first planning round. After every CEM iteration, DINO-WM\'s evaluator executes the current mean plan in the simulator, which gives us a success rate and a final distance to the goal per iteration. Grey lines are the three seeds, blue is their mean, orange is the paper\'s single-plan CEM result. Success rises steeply for about ten iterations, from 0.05 to 0.76, then flattens; from iteration 22 to 30 the mean does not change. The distance on the right keeps creeping down slightly, which says the plans are still getting a little closer without crossing the success threshold.',
      takeaway: 'Most of CEM\'s benefit comes early; the last eight iterations, about 45 minutes per round on our GPU, added no success in these runs.',
      visual: 'Left: success rate per iteration (0 to 1). Right: mean final state distance in simulator units, lower is better. No smoothing is applied; each point is a measurement over 50 episodes.',
      why: 'This curve tells us how much compute budget we can trade for faster development runs, and it is the reference for checking whether gating slows CEM down.',
      careful: 'This is test-time optimisation, not a training curve: no weights change. The per-iteration success is privileged evaluation information that the planner does not use (apart from upstream\'s rule that stops CEM when every episode already succeeds, which never triggered here). State distance includes velocities, so it can stay large even on a success. The orange line is the paper\'s final single-plan number, not a curve from the paper.',
      q: 'Why not just stop at iteration 22?',
      a: 'For development runs we will use fewer iterations to save time. For reported results we keep the upstream 30 so that our baseline equals the published protocol; the plateau is one configuration and three seeds, not a general rule.',
